Below is a minimal, **self‑contained** example that shows how to write and run unit tests with **xUnit.NET** in C#.  
It contains:

1. A small `Calculator` class (the “SUT” – System Under Test).  
2. A test project that references the library and contains xUnit tests.  
3. The `*.csproj` files for both projects so you can compile/run the tests with the dotnet CLI.

> **Tip** – All the files can be created in a single folder (or separate folders).  
> Run `dotnet test` from the solution root (or the test project folder) to execute the tests.

---

## 1. The library (Calculator.cs)

```csharp
// File: Calculator.cs
namespace MyMathLib;

public class Calculator
{
    public int Add(int a, int b) => a + b;

    public int Subtract(int a, int b) => a - b;

    public int Multiply(int a, int b) => a * b;

    public double Divide(int a, int b)
    {
        if (b == 0) throw new DivideByZeroException();
        return (double)a / b;
    }
}
```

---

## 2. The test project

### 2.1 Project file (`MyMathLib.Tests.csproj`)

```xml
<!-- File: MyMathLib.Tests.csproj -->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Reference the library you want to test -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\MyMathLib\MyMathLib.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- xUnit runtime and test runner -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.6.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.6.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.10.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
```

> **Why these packages?**  
> * `xunit` – the assertion library and attributes (`Fact`, `Theory`, …).  
> * `xunit.runner.visualstudio` – lets Visual Studio or the dotnet CLI discover and run the tests.  
> * `Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk` – the test SDK that orchestrates the test host.

### 2.2 The test class (`CalculatorTests.cs`)

```csharp
// File: CalculatorTests.cs
using Xunit;
using MyMathLib; // namespace of the class under test

namespace MyMathLib.Tests;

public class CalculatorTests
{
    // Arrange: a single shared instance is fine for this simple example
    private readonly Calculator _calc = new();

    // 1. A classic unit test (non‑parameterized)
    [Fact] // <-- runs once
    public void Add_ReturnsCorrectSum()
    {
        // Act
        int result = _calc.Add(3, 4);

        // Assert
        Assert.Equal(7, result);
    }

    // 2. Parameterized test (Theory + InlineData)
    [Theory]
    [InlineData(10, 5, 5)]
    [InlineData(7, 3, 4)]
    [InlineData(-3, -7, -10)]
    public void Subtract_ReturnsCorrectDifference(int a, int b, int expected)
    {
        int result = _calc.Subtract(a, b);
        Assert.Equal(expected, result);
    }

    // 3. Test for exception throwing
    [Fact]
    public void Divide_ByZero_ThrowsDivideByZeroException()
    {
        // Act & Assert combined – we expect the lambda to throw
        Assert.Throws<DivideByZeroException>(() => _calc.Divide(10, 0));
    }

    // 4. Another parameterized test
    [Theory]
    [InlineData(4, 2, 8)]
    [InlineData(6, 3, 18)]
    public void Multiply_ReturnsCorrectProduct(int a, int b, int expected)
    {
        int result = _calc.Multiply(a, b);
        Assert.Equal(expected, result);
    }
}
```

> **What you see:**
> * `[Fact]` – a single, non‑parameterized test.  
> * `[Theory]` – a data‑driven test that runs once per data row.  
> * `InlineData` supplies the arguments.  
> * `Assert.Equal` verifies the result.  
> * `Assert.Throws` checks that an exception is raised.

---

## 3. Build & run

```bash
# From the folder that contains MyMathLib.Tests.csproj (or a solution that includes both projects)
dotnet test
```

The output will show something like:

```
Test Run Successful. Time elapsed: 00:00:01.234
          Passed: 4, Failed: 0, Skipped: 0, Total: 4
```

All four tests are executed, confirming that the `Calculator` behaves as expected.

---

## 4. (Optional) Async example

If you want to test an `async` method, simply mark the test method with `async Task` and use `await` inside:

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task AsyncMethod_ReturnsExpected()
{
    var result = await SomeAsyncMethod();
    Assert.True(result);
}
```

xUnit automatically supports async tests – no special attributes required.

---

### TL;DR

1. Create a normal class library for the code you want to test.  
2. Create a separate test project, reference the library, add xUnit packages.  
3. Write tests using `[Fact]`, `[Theory]`, `Assert.*`.  
4. Run with `dotnet test`.

Happy testing!
